WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:04.643
- Hi everyone.

00:00:04.643 --> 00:00:07.100
Welcome to the daily homeroom live stream,

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.640
Sal here from Khan Academy.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.950
For those of you all who are new to this,

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.180
this is a live stream that
we've been doing every day

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.270
since we've had these
global school closures.

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:16.610
Just as a way to stay connected.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:19.270
Obviously Khan Academy, our whole team,

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:20.936
we were trying to pull out resources,

00:00:20.936 --> 00:00:23.460
starting in early learning
with Khan Academy kids

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.250
all the way through elementary,
middle and high school

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.750
and even early college
to keep you learning.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.330
But we also want to provide more support.

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.580
So we've been running paired
webinars, teacher webinars,

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.040
this homeroom live stream,
where we're going to have

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.130
really interesting guests including,

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:38.260
today I'm especially excited about.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.362
But I do want to give my
a standard announcement

00:00:40.362 --> 00:00:43.210
reminding folks that
we are not for profit.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:46.390
We only can exist because
of philanthropic donations.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:49.090
We were running at a deficit
even before COVID hit.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.300
And now with COVID,
we're seeing our traffic,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.380
three X of what it typically is.

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:55.500
We are trying to do more programs

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.310
and ways to support you.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:58.370
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.510
please think about donating.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.760
I want to give special thanks
to several corporations

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.809
that have stepped up really in record time

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:05.610
to support this effort.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.000
Bank of America, that
first weekend when they saw

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.480
that we needed, they
stepped up to help us out.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:14.618
Google.org, AT&amp;T, Fastly and
Novartis, thank all of them.

00:01:14.618 --> 00:01:16.910
And even with their significant support,

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:17.920
we still need more help.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.810
So if you represent a
corporation, please talk to us.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:22.460
And if you're just an individual donor,

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.170
that still makes a huge,
huge, huge difference,

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.935
for our ability to serve
tens of millions of folks

00:01:27.935 --> 00:01:29.520
around the world.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.570
So with that, I am super
excited to introduce our guest,

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.480
Angela Duckworth.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:39.080
In certain circles, she is
a mega star, I would say.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.580
I've known Angela, for many years.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.750
I think we ran into each other
at a conference in Canada.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.606
This was like eight,
nine, or 10 years ago,

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:48.930
eight or nine years ago.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:53.757
But, then I, even then I knew
Angela was on onto something

00:01:53.757 --> 00:01:57.700
and since then I think her
notoriety has just exploded

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.460
because the things she
talks about are so relevant

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.140
to education, I think, especially relevant

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.070
to the time that we're
dealing with right now.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:07.030
So, Angela, thank you for joining us.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:09.080
- Thank you Sal, and I'm
going to tell you a secret.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.270
You didn't just run into me.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:13.530
I actually flew to Canada because I knew

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.210
someone had told me that
you were going to be

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:19.010
and I ran into you but not by accident.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:20.303
So I was stalking you.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:23.750
- We see, this is how
the tables have turned

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:25.707
because now I kind of stalk you.

00:02:25.707 --> 00:02:26.769
(laughing)

00:02:26.769 --> 00:02:27.620
- You just email me.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:28.453
It's a lot easier for you,

00:02:28.453 --> 00:02:30.450
you didn't have to like fly to Canada.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.050
- Exactly. I didn't.

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:36.320
So I'm glad that we are
mutually stalking each other.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:39.080
And I'm going to start
off the conversation,

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:40.780
but I do want to encourage
everyone who's watching

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:44.381
on YouTube and Facebook, put
questions in there for Angela,

00:02:44.381 --> 00:02:46.300
and you're going to learn a lot more about

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:49.960
her areas of expertise if you
don't already know of them.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:53.260
But I will start, Angela, you're known,

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.690
you're one of really a handful
of, I would say, pioneers

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:59.600
or leaders in this field
of mindset research.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.290
And you're really known for grit.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.730
Defining it in more precise terms.

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:08.740
So just, you know, explain
what mindset research is

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:12.223
and your view on it,
especially relative to grit.

00:03:13.191 --> 00:03:15.280
- The term mindset has caught on.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.780
I have to think that
it's gotta be almost the,

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.530
maybe the most commonly understood
new scientific discovery

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:25.530
in psychology and among
non psychologists, right?

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.450
Among parents and kids and educators.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:29.490
So what is mindset?

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:33.770
You know, mindsets are beliefs
that you have about the world

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:38.570
or about human nature that
drive so much of your behavior.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:42.250
They may not be beliefs that
you think about actively,

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:43.450
but they're very powerful.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:45.620
And in particular Carol Dweck at Stanford,

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:49.300
our common friend and really my hero,

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.440
she's worked her whole
life on something called

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:53.390
growth mindset.

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:55.930
Your beliefs about your intelligence.

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:58.330
And you can either have a growth mindset,

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:00.250
which is you know, the
belief that intelligence

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:02.570
is malleable, that your
abilities can change,

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:05.149
or you can have a fixed
mindset, which is the belief

00:04:05.149 --> 00:04:07.870
that they can't change that
you know, you are who you are,

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:08.703
who you are.

00:04:08.703 --> 00:04:10.130
You're either a math person or not,

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:11.963
you're a natural athlete or not.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.970
And in my research on grit,

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:19.230
which is passionate and persevering effort

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:22.720
towards longterm goals,
I find, and I think

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:25.559
our early work with Carol, but
in subsequent studies also,

00:04:25.559 --> 00:04:29.016
kids who have a growth
mindset about intelligence

00:04:29.016 --> 00:04:31.840
tend to become grittier.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.740
And then those increases
in grit tend to lead

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.510
to increases in growth mindset.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.070
So there's a kind of virtuous cycle,

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.320
when a kid really believes that
their abilities can change,

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:44.900
they're more likely to
persevere, to try hard at things,

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:48.008
which then reinforces that belief.

00:04:48.008 --> 00:04:50.610
- And just to make sure
I understand these,

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:52.420
because I often say these
in the same sentence

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:54.890
and they are very closely
related to growth mindset is,

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:56.990
I believe that I can
do more, I just have to

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:00.040
step out of my comfort
zone and apply myself.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.110
And if I fail at something,
it's not a judgment

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:03.680
on some type of innate ability.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:06.430
It's if it's a moment to learn from.

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:09.343
While grit is that ability to stick to it.

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:14.220
- Yeah, I would say grit,
I mean grit is a behavior,

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:15.900
grit is like doing it right?

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.420
And mindset might be why
you're doing it, right?

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:21.030
So mindset is like the underlying belief

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:23.560
that would motivate a
certain pattern of behavior

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:26.280
that I would describe as grit.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.610
And let me just say, because you know,

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:30.410
my kids are still teenagers and you know,

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:32.020
I'm sure a lot of people have kids who are

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:34.097
even younger than, than ours.

00:05:34.097 --> 00:05:37.430
I think that it's not
just, when I think of grit,

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:40.100
I don't think just of like working hard.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:41.540
It's also loving what you do.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:44.650
It's also being intrinsically motivated

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:46.670
over long periods of time.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:50.370
So, that's just a part of
grit that I hope, you know,

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:51.610
we can talk about in this conversation

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:54.108
because I don't want parents
to get the message that like

00:05:54.108 --> 00:05:57.130
the only thing kids need to
do is develop a work ethic.

00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:00.753
They also have developed
interests, loves, passions.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:03.210
- [Sal] Yeah, and that's,
I think, a good segue.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.260
I mean, you know, you're
a professor of psychology

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:06.110
at University of Pennsylvania.

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:07.880
You've been doing research for a while.

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:11.521
You have a bestselling book
named, not a surprise, Grit.

00:06:11.521 --> 00:06:12.910
(laughing)

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:15.640
- Yeah. No imagination there in the title.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:18.890
- No, it's the power of
passion and perseverance.

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:20.390
I think, you know, a question that

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:21.850
a lot of parents are asking.

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:24.550
My wife asked me to ask
you this when she saw that

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:25.980
I was walking into the walk-in closet

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:27.740
to interview you right now.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.160
And it's a question
that has been coming up

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:30.850
throughout the school closures,

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:32.380
is that there's a lot
of resources out there.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:35.610
There's Khan Academy, we've
put out daily schedules.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:38.840
But what is tangible advice for parents,

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:42.450
teachers, or students for
themselves to have that grit?

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:47.280
To be able to act on that
growth mindset, so to speak?

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:50.540
- So I think that, not
only for grit but for

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:53.420
lots of other things that we
would love our kids to develop

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:57.580
as they grow older, the combination of

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:01.370
challenge and support is magical.

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:03.561
And, if you ask the question like,

00:07:03.561 --> 00:07:05.430
what are good parents, right?

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:07.180
Like what does the
science of parenting say?

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:10.830
There's now decades of
research on parenting styles

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:14.450
and the style of parenting
that is the best, really.

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:16.720
I mean, I can just say that
flat out like it's the best.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:18.030
Your kids will be happier,

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.710
they'll be more socially adjusted,

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:23.410
but they'll also be more accomplished.

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:27.510
Like it's this combination
of being very supportive

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:29.520
but also being demanding.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.520
So you could say it's tough love.

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:32.980
Now why do I bring that up now

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:34.530
in the middle of the COVID crisis?

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:36.790
Because kids are being challenged.

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:40.630
So their challenges have
been, I think in all cases

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:43.540
and in many different ways raised, right?

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:47.370
Now the question is like, how
as parents can you meet that

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:49.550
with increased support?

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:52.080
Not trivial because we're under
our own stress, et cetera.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.090
But if you ask the question to me,

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:56.590
like where does psychological
growth come from?

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:59.330
It comes from this combination of like,

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:01.810
I'm being asked to do
something I can't yet do,

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:04.140
but there's a floor beneath
me, there's a foundation,

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:07.210
there's unconditional support,
there is Khan Academy there,

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.681
you know, there are people
who will help me, you know,

00:08:09.681 --> 00:08:11.790
like make a schedule so
I can go on Khan Academy.

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:16.000
So I think parents can
respond to this crisis

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:18.099
by in some ways, thinking
of it as an opportunity

00:08:18.099 --> 00:08:21.040
for their kids to develop these qualities.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:22.780
I call them character strengths,

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:24.700
but you can call them SEL skills,

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:26.210
you can call them anything you want.

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:29.520
And I really do think
it's not too Pollyanna

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:33.089
to say that it is an opportunity
to do all those things.

00:08:33.089 --> 00:08:34.370
- And what would you, I mean, one,

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:37.207
it's impressive that there is, I mean,

00:08:37.207 --> 00:08:38.910
people have done the research
and they've been able

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:40.393
to categorize different styles and this,

00:08:40.393 --> 00:08:43.067
what you're describing seems
to be the winning style on,

00:08:43.067 --> 00:08:46.190
and I'm personally insecure
to see how well I'm,

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:48.853
I don't know what audits--

00:08:48.853 --> 00:08:49.686
- Give you a quiz.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:53.170
- Give me a quiz or watch our family.

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:56.013
But what either from
your or just as a parent

00:08:56.013 --> 00:08:58.090
that you see good practice,
I know y'all have done

00:08:58.090 --> 00:08:59.860
a lot of research on interventions

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:02.538
that can help change people's mindsets.

00:09:02.538 --> 00:09:05.430
So I mean, one, I'd love
to hear about that research

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:08.354
and then are there things,
let's say hypothetically

00:09:08.354 --> 00:09:10.930
you were to have a five
and a half year old at home

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:12.215
or an eight year old.

00:09:12.215 --> 00:09:13.048
- For example.

00:09:13.048 --> 00:09:14.140
- For example, I don't know.

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:15.360
Some people I know.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.930
And you know, they sometimes do their work

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:19.250
but they're maybe just doing the minimum

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:20.740
or sometimes they don't
even want to do that

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:21.830
because they feel like there's a little

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:23.810
less accountability now.

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:25.590
What does the research
tell us and what advice

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:28.260
would you have for this
hypothetical person that I know?

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.510
- Yeah. Well you know, I
have a non hypothetical

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:33.210
17 and non hypothetical 18 year old,

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:36.450
but you know, long hypothetical
five and a half year old

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.360
it sounds like on your end.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:42.590
So I think one of the
challenges of parenting

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:45.250
is to always be titrating
to where your kids are.

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:47.020
Because when I said that you need to be,

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:48.590
and I do believe this, like you need to be

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:49.960
a demanding parent.

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:52.900
I don't think kids, I
mean, kids need parents,

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:55.620
like they need us to ask them to do things

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:58.776
that they can't yet or
wouldn't on their own do.

00:09:58.776 --> 00:10:03.320
But the titration part is, you
know, how much is a stretch

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:07.030
for your kid at this
particular moment in time

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:09.120
and also at this stage
in their development?

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:11.490
It may not be the same as it is

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:13.780
even for another kid in the same family.

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:17.150
So the titration is how to have your kids,

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:20.130
you know, be challenged but not too much.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:23.310
And so for example, you
know, when parents have

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:26.340
a idealistic goal of like
their kids waking up,

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:30.090
making a schedule for the
day, sticking to the schedule,

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:34.950
you know, if you start with
a really unrealistic goal,

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:37.898
and you ask your kid
to get there, you know,

00:10:37.898 --> 00:10:41.004
immediately then everybody
just gets discouraged

00:10:41.004 --> 00:10:42.400
and frustrated.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:47.010
So one piece of parenting
advice is to take stock

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:50.570
of where your kids are and then
try to move like 10%. Right?

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:52.638
As opposed to like a hundred percent,

00:10:52.638 --> 00:10:56.460
towards something that
you think is better.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.470
And in general I find that
even adults procrastinate

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:03.520
and don't do well when they
fail to break down big goals

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:06.288
into smaller parts and then, you know,

00:11:06.288 --> 00:11:09.580
make a more reasonable
schedule about, you know,

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:13.273
how they're going to accomplish,
you know, go down the list.

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:15.230
- That's good advice.

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:16.210
Not just for our parenting.

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:18.010
I mean, just while you were saying that,

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:19.863
I'm just, I'm thinking
about some of the things

00:11:19.863 --> 00:11:22.340
that my wife and I have attempted to do,

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:23.530
especially with some of our younger kids

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:25.540
and I was like, yeah, maybe
if we just broke that down

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:28.260
a little bit more or just
expected the first step of that.

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:29.093
And then once--

00:11:29.093 --> 00:11:30.870
- I mean, Sal, this is
what you do so well.

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:33.290
Like, by the way, I mean, I will just say

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:35.814
you have taught my kids since, you know,

00:11:35.814 --> 00:11:38.270
as you do so many kids around the world.

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:41.910
And you may not have known,
but the reason I flew to Canada

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:44.160
to stalk you was because
my kids were spending

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:46.740
so much time learning
from you on Khan Academy.

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:49.700
And I just got really
interested in, you know,

00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:51.877
how psychologists like me and
might learn something from you

00:11:51.877 --> 00:11:53.170
and be helpful.

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:55.390
And I think what you do so well Sal,

00:11:55.390 --> 00:12:00.169
is you break things down into like parts.

00:12:00.169 --> 00:12:02.350
So that like you can
swallow it kind of like

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:03.600
a teaspoon at a time.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:07.020
And I think kids need our
help as parents to help them,

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:08.050
you know, do that.

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:10.090
And I sometimes feel like, you know,

00:12:10.090 --> 00:12:12.437
parents might have unrealistic
expectations about,

00:12:12.437 --> 00:12:16.570
you know, like how much progress
they can expect their kid

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:18.310
to make when it comes to self regulation

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:21.043
or time management or discipline.

00:12:21.043 --> 00:12:24.500
And it helps to remember
that the great teachers

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:26.430
know to break things down.

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:28.670
- No, well, I think you're,
you're being very flattering.

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:30.960
But, I think the point is right.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:34.080
And I think as parents, as
much as we try to be centered,

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:36.130
we can also sometimes feel an urgency.

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:39.840
Like, wait, I heard someone
else's kid has, you know,

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:42.600
has their whole schedule, is
doing everything perfectly

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:45.280
and you're probably doing
the wrong thing to your kid

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:47.577
and you're trying to get
them from zero to that

00:12:47.577 --> 00:12:49.740
and that other child
probably isn't doing that.

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:52.633
There's probably a little bit
of marketing involved there.

00:12:52.633 --> 00:12:54.780
- I think we probably all
have stories of, you know,

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:55.718
childhood in my family,

00:12:55.718 --> 00:12:59.600
it was always these like cousins
that I have in Boston who,

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:01.290
you know, were seemingly perfect.

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:02.740
And actually now that I'm all grown up,

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:04.440
I realized they were pretty much perfect

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:07.540
and they all became, you
know, like Harvard professors.

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:08.373
But I don't think that--

00:13:08.373 --> 00:13:11.155
- I'm afraid, if you're the
non-perfect one in the family,

00:13:11.155 --> 00:13:12.200
who knows what they're doing?

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:14.840
- Oh, I think I'm the black sheep.

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:16.910
But yes, there's a very
distinguished branch of our family,

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:18.350
but I'm not in it.

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:21.710
So, you know, why do parents
make those comparisons?

00:13:21.710 --> 00:13:23.800
I think they're just, you
know, they're kind of just

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:25.433
what come to mind for the
parent because they, they are,

00:13:25.433 --> 00:13:27.767
you know, like, "Oh,
that's the mental model

00:13:27.767 --> 00:13:29.800
"I have in my head, so I'll
just share it with you."

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:31.440
But I don't think kids benefit very much

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:32.750
from those kinds of comparisons.

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:34.880
And in general in schooling, I think,

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:36.800
and I'm thinking, I know
you have a global audience,

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:38.810
but I'm thinking about the
American schooling system.

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:40.910
You know, it's a very kind of rank order.

00:13:40.910 --> 00:13:45.240
Like how are you relative
to these other like 900 kids

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:46.100
who are your age?

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:47.830
And those kinds of comparisons,

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:50.831
like am I as smart as her,
am I smarter than him?

00:13:50.831 --> 00:13:53.730
I don't think they're super
beneficial most of the time.

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:55.360
I think kids actually benefit from make

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:58.776
a different kind of
comparison, which is how am I

00:13:58.776 --> 00:14:02.860
compared to how I want to be
compared to how I was. Right?

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:05.110
And that's a comparison that
you can do something with.

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:07.110
And that's a comparison that you know,

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:10.870
doesn't diminish another person
by yourself making progress.

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:12.930
And you know, when I
think about Khan Academy

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:15.280
and your whole philosophy is, you know,

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:17.220
you have a book too,
which I read and you know,

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:20.300
your whole philosophy of like
how education ought to work.

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:24.020
I really do think it's,
you know, it's you with you

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:27.040
and like how far you can go
and not you and your cousins

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:31.410
in Boston or you and
you know, the 899 kids

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:34.200
who are also in your graduating class.

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:36.460
- And I mean with that thought,

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:37.680
there's a ton of questions coming in.

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:38.620
I want to get to those questions,

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:42.030
but I'll ask one of my last
indulgent Sal questions,

00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:43.677
which is, you talked
a lot of research that

00:14:43.677 --> 00:14:45.680
you can change people's mindsets.

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:47.880
I mean, tell us a little bit
about some of the research

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:51.430
that will give all of us
a growth mindset about

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:54.090
changing our kids' mindset about grit.

00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:55.440
- Well, I'll tell you about a big study

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:58.375
that was run recently
and published in Nature.

00:14:58.375 --> 00:15:01.720
It was a huge scientific
team led by David Yeager

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:04.830
at UT Austin and also, of
course, Carol Dweck was involved.

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:07.130
I was just one among many team members.

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:10.010
But what it was, was an experiment to see

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:13.360
whether we could increase growth mindset

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:16.460
in ninth graders all
over the United States.

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:18.330
And the experiment was a success.

00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:20.100
And if you ask the question
like what did you do?

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:21.840
How did you increase growth mindset?

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:26.130
There were these brief really,
just, you know, minutes long,

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:30.464
activities where students A,
learned that lots of other kids

00:15:30.464 --> 00:15:34.780
around their age, you know,
also sometimes feel stupid

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:38.120
when they get something wrong
and they can kind of slip into

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:41.177
a like, "Oh, I'll never learn this," mode.

00:15:41.177 --> 00:15:44.220
But then they learned
about the neuroscience

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:47.270
that's actually true,
that the brain is plastic,

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:48.640
that there is tremendous growth.

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:51.590
And when you do struggle
and you make a mistake

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:54.820
and you look at it again, that
your brain actually changes.

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:57.805
So for me, when I think about this,

00:15:57.805 --> 00:16:00.350
one of the reasons why
intelligence is a hard thing

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:02.690
to get kids to understand
like that it is malleable,

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:04.050
is because it's invisible.

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:06.840
It's like it's in your, you
know, you can't see your brain.

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:08.797
Right? Whereas if you said to a kid like,

00:16:08.797 --> 00:16:10.227
"Do you think if you
lifted all these weights,

00:16:10.227 --> 00:16:12.020
"you know, your biceps would get bigger?"

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:13.100
They would say like, "Of course."

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:15.960
But intelligence is
kind of behind you know,

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:17.880
the forehead so you don't see it.

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:21.470
Anyway, ninth graders learned
about the modern neuroscience

00:16:21.470 --> 00:16:23.000
of brain plasticity.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:25.010
And then the last thing that they did was

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:28.750
they actually wrote a letter
to other students that were,

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:31.130
you know, for example, like
a little younger than you.

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:31.963
Like what would you say?

00:16:31.963 --> 00:16:33.870
How would you summarize
all of this for them?

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:37.640
And I think this
combination of feeling like,

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:41.350
oh, I hear the voices of
other students who you know,

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:42.990
can sometimes feel dumb
when they do something,

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:45.290
but then they learn to not feel that way,

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:47.940
to understand mistakes
as growth and learning.

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:50.170
You get some scientific evidence

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:52.210
and then you are put in the position of

00:16:52.210 --> 00:16:54.150
communicating that to other students.

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:56.910
I think it's quite brilliant honestly

00:16:56.910 --> 00:17:00.510
and in this study there
were measurable increases

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:02.560
in mindset, growth mindset.

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:07.441
And then also, particularly
for students who had schools

00:17:07.441 --> 00:17:09.880
where the cultures supported it.

00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:13.350
There were, also improvements in grades.

00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:15.246
- Wow. Just to make that very--

00:17:15.246 --> 00:17:17.010
- Actually, I should say test scores.

00:17:17.010 --> 00:17:19.610
I think there was like, I
should like check on that.

00:17:19.610 --> 00:17:21.310
I think it was like math
grades and test scores.

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:22.143
But yeah.

00:17:22.143 --> 00:17:24.100
- No, but either way,
that's very compelling.

00:17:24.100 --> 00:17:28.900
I mean, are there ways for
parents to get that intervention

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:31.170
for either themselves or their kids?

00:17:31.170 --> 00:17:33.580
Yeah, I'll make sure that
you all have the hyperlink

00:17:33.580 --> 00:17:36.840
to the work and some of
this work was supported

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:38.340
by the Mindset Scholars Network.

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:41.640
And you know, there's a
link where you can actually

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:43.010
get this also for educators.

00:17:43.010 --> 00:17:46.093
I mean, I'll make sure that
you have the right resources.

00:17:46.093 --> 00:17:48.670
And finally I'll just say, I think parents

00:17:48.670 --> 00:17:50.080
should read Carol's book.

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:51.390
I mean, they should read your book too.

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:52.230
They should read lots of books.

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:54.750
But I think Mindset is terrific

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:57.303
and it's beautifully written.

00:17:58.680 --> 00:17:59.513
- Good advice.

00:17:59.513 --> 00:18:01.337
So there's a lot of questions here.

00:18:01.337 --> 00:18:04.827
So from YouTube, Joe asks, "Angela,

00:18:04.827 --> 00:18:07.637
"can you use grit to force
yourself to have a passion

00:18:07.637 --> 00:18:09.807
"for something you have
little interest in?

00:18:09.807 --> 00:18:11.347
"For example, a job?"

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:14.959
- No. If you want more
than a one word answer,

00:18:14.959 --> 00:18:19.250
I can elaborate, but I don't think that

00:18:19.250 --> 00:18:23.160
you can force yourself to
be passionate about things.

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:25.710
So when I measure intrinsic motivation,

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:28.700
feeling genuinely curious about things

00:18:28.700 --> 00:18:30.750
and also feeling like your work is aligned

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:32.262
with your personal values,

00:18:32.262 --> 00:18:35.430
that is what's correlated with grit.

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:38.750
Not extrinsic, you know,
like I was coerced to do it

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:40.660
or I'll make a lot of money if I do it.

00:18:40.660 --> 00:18:44.530
So I think for me the advice would not be

00:18:44.530 --> 00:18:48.330
to try to force yourself
but actually to find

00:18:48.330 --> 00:18:50.210
if you're already an
adult, like to find a job

00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:52.530
that is more closely aligned
with your developed interests

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:53.363
and your values.

00:18:53.363 --> 00:18:55.918
And if you're still a kid,
I would say, you know,

00:18:55.918 --> 00:18:59.530
the word you want to use
about with passion is develop.

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:03.160
I think you want to develop
passions that are mature

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:06.100
and so that requires, you
know, lots and lots of time,

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:08.330
you know, doing things
becoming more and more

00:19:08.330 --> 00:19:10.460
and more interested in
them and committed to them.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:13.040
But I don't think it's a
process of forcing yourself

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:15.283
or for anybody else to force you either.

00:19:16.260 --> 00:19:18.010
- And this is really interesting
because, so you know, grit.

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:20.040
When people think of the word
just in the colloquial sense,

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:22.576
they think of like being
able to power through pain--

00:19:22.576 --> 00:19:23.607
- I know.

00:19:23.607 --> 00:19:25.130
- As one form of grit.

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:26.330
But that's not what you're saying.

00:19:26.330 --> 00:19:28.090
- Which is the wrong word.

00:19:28.090 --> 00:19:29.750
- No, it's a great word.

00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:32.090
You can exhibit the qualities of grit,

00:19:32.090 --> 00:19:34.680
but it's not because you're
powering through pain,

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.270
it's because you are
intrinsically motivated

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:37.790
in what you're doing.

00:19:37.790 --> 00:19:39.700
- Yeah. And I don't
want to paint this like,

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:41.730
you know, look, you work really hard, Sal.

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:42.563
I work really hard.

00:19:42.563 --> 00:19:44.010
I think there are parts of our day

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:45.930
where we don't want to
do what we have to do,

00:19:45.930 --> 00:19:46.763
but we have to do it.

00:19:46.763 --> 00:19:49.413
But I would say about myself and I'm sure

00:19:49.413 --> 00:19:52.160
that you would say it about
like, you love what you do,

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:54.140
you love what you do.

00:19:54.140 --> 00:19:57.840
And so yeah, there's practice
and there's hard work

00:19:57.840 --> 00:19:59.540
and there's getting over disappointment.

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:02.670
Nobody loves doing it, but
overall you love what you do

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:04.830
and I think that's the
part that's missing in that

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:08.180
one syllable word that
it doesn't sound like it.

00:20:08.180 --> 00:20:11.423
But when I study it and when I find it

00:20:11.423 --> 00:20:14.533
in a really high performer,
it's really always there.

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:17.490
- You know, I'll throw
out a hack that I think

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:19.790
is related to Joe's question
and I think it's consistent

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:21.890
with what you said, but I
have no scientific backing

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:24.510
other than my N of one and
what's going on in my own

00:20:24.510 --> 00:20:28.920
bizarre head, which is one
of my skills that I have

00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:31.480
and I'm quite proud of it,
is I can convince myself

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:33.970
to be passionate about almost anything.

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:35.240
- Is that right?

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:36.630
Wait, give me an example.

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:38.680
- Oh, almost any I, you know, well,

00:20:38.680 --> 00:20:39.880
I mean, a lot of people in Khan Academy,

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:41.840
they can hear it in my voice
sometimes in these videos,

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:44.150
like I'm excited about the Aldol reaction

00:20:44.150 --> 00:20:44.983
and they're like, why?

00:20:44.983 --> 00:20:46.840
I was like, well, I
don't even make the video

00:20:46.840 --> 00:20:48.420
until I am excited about it

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:50.230
because there is something exciting.

00:20:50.230 --> 00:20:52.820
You know, in academics,
what I remind students

00:20:52.820 --> 00:20:55.340
is almost everything is the culmination

00:20:55.340 --> 00:20:57.150
of someone's life work.

00:20:57.150 --> 00:21:00.900
And they would have
killed to have the answers

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:02.130
that you have in your textbook

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:05.370
or that are being explained to
you on a Khan Academy video.

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:07.970
And that by itself makes it exciting.

00:21:07.970 --> 00:21:10.020
You know, and even
things as part of my job

00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:11.730
that aren't like my
favorite thing, you know,

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:13.710
I love our auditors, they're great people,

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:15.640
but you know, sitting in like a meeting

00:21:15.640 --> 00:21:18.300
about our audit report
isn't like, you know what,

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:20.640
I clicked my heels on
it, but I remind myself,

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:23.690
no, this isn't a very
important step in order for us

00:21:23.690 --> 00:21:26.610
to become a real
organization in order for us

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:27.570
to serve more people.

00:21:27.570 --> 00:21:30.990
So I definitely do that, I remind myself

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:33.200
why what I'm doing matters.
- Yes.

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:36.220
- And, you know, I had a
boss that told me my first,

00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:37.960
you know, I was working at a big company

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:39.977
and there's times when I was
like, "Oh, it doesn't matter

00:21:39.977 --> 00:21:43.020
"if I show up or not, I'm kind
of a cog in this big thing."

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:45.387
And he said, "Look, you
just have to show up

00:21:45.387 --> 00:21:48.497
"and make yourself and make
the company better off today

00:21:48.497 --> 00:21:50.200
"than it was yesterday."

00:21:50.200 --> 00:21:51.740
And there's something about
that that made me feel

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:52.960
very motivated about it.

00:21:52.960 --> 00:21:55.277
And I'm like, "Oh yeah,
yeah, I can get passionate

00:21:55.277 --> 00:21:56.161
"about that."

00:21:56.161 --> 00:21:56.994
But I think we're consistent.

00:21:56.994 --> 00:21:59.970
As long as you can get yourself
authentically passionate,

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:03.520
motivated, then you can,
the grit will kind of happen

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:04.770
or can happen.

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:07.020
- Yeah. And okay, so you said,

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:08.360
oh, this is your N of one.

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:10.900
Like, let me give you a
little science behind that.

00:22:10.900 --> 00:22:13.950
So, when you think
about why you have to go

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:16.130
to an auditing meeting or think about kids

00:22:16.130 --> 00:22:17.190
doing their homework, right?

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:19.427
I mean, so many kids feel
like, "Why am I doing this?

00:22:19.427 --> 00:22:20.819
"This is totally pointless."

00:22:20.819 --> 00:22:22.680
What they're feeling is that there isn't

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:24.170
any purpose to their work.

00:22:24.170 --> 00:22:27.500
There isn't any like this is
a means, but what's the end?

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:30.090
And so what your maybe your boss did,

00:22:30.090 --> 00:22:32.490
and certainly what human
beings do is it's useful

00:22:32.490 --> 00:22:34.690
to think about your goals hierarchically.

00:22:34.690 --> 00:22:37.670
Like, you know, why am
I on this video with

00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:39.410
Khan Academy, with Sal Khan?

00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:41.320
It's like, because actually I believe that

00:22:41.320 --> 00:22:43.240
psychological science
can help kids thrive.

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:45.550
So for me, this time that
we're spending together

00:22:45.550 --> 00:22:46.610
has a purpose.

00:22:46.610 --> 00:22:48.950
If a kid is doing their
homework and they see the point

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:51.730
of doing these, you know,
practice problems or like,

00:22:51.730 --> 00:22:54.270
and they see the point
of this class overall,

00:22:54.270 --> 00:22:56.530
it's really like what you're
doing is you're helping them

00:22:56.530 --> 00:22:59.450
see the kind of the goal
that sits above this goal.

00:22:59.450 --> 00:23:01.437
Like, "Oh, this is the
reason why I'm doing it.

00:23:01.437 --> 00:23:03.430
"The hierarchy makes sense to me."

00:23:03.430 --> 00:23:06.170
So I agree with you
that sometimes we forget

00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:07.700
and then we have to remind ourselves

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:10.150
and then it gives meaning
and purpose to something

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:12.533
that otherwise would be mundane.

00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:15.650
But I guess I wouldn't go so far as

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:17.020
recommending to people that you could

00:23:17.020 --> 00:23:18.830
make yourself passionate about everything.

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:20.197
I don't think that's what you're saying,

00:23:20.197 --> 00:23:23.010
because I don't think, I
don't think it's possible

00:23:23.010 --> 00:23:25.730
for people to like really
literally be randomly assigned

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:27.540
to a job and then just be,

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:30.350
'cause I think about your
life, Sal, and I think,

00:23:30.350 --> 00:23:33.610
you know, obviously it
took an unexpected turn

00:23:33.610 --> 00:23:35.360
when you left, you know, your career track

00:23:35.360 --> 00:23:37.420
and you became who you are today.

00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:39.670
But I also have to believe that, you know,

00:23:39.670 --> 00:23:41.700
I couldn't just like
have randomly selected

00:23:41.700 --> 00:23:44.571
any career path for you that
you would be working as hard

00:23:44.571 --> 00:23:49.170
as you are now, for Khan Academy.

00:23:49.170 --> 00:23:50.610
- Oh, for sure, for sure.

00:23:50.610 --> 00:23:52.070
I mean there's definitely an optimization.

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:54.920
When I have a choice, I
definitely am optimizing

00:23:54.920 --> 00:23:57.640
for what fills my bucket, so to speak.

00:23:57.640 --> 00:23:59.955
And I'm learning a lot of
five-year-old language.

00:23:59.955 --> 00:24:00.802
(laughing)

00:24:00.802 --> 00:24:02.243
But what fills my bucket.

00:24:02.243 --> 00:24:05.063
But no, it's an interesting,
I mean I have to ponder that.

00:24:05.063 --> 00:24:07.340
I have to meditate on that more to what

00:24:07.340 --> 00:24:10.020
am I living in my own delusion or not?

00:24:10.020 --> 00:24:11.990
- I think you've made it,

00:24:11.990 --> 00:24:14.300
here's how I just taught
this undergraduate class

00:24:14.300 --> 00:24:19.100
called Grit Lab to 60
undergraduates at my university,

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:20.390
University of Pennsylvania.

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:22.750
And when I had to
summarize the entire class

00:24:22.750 --> 00:24:25.167
in the last hour of the class, I said,

00:24:25.167 --> 00:24:26.527
"All right, three things.

00:24:26.527 --> 00:24:31.090
"Choose easy, work hard, pay it forward."

00:24:31.090 --> 00:24:33.680
And the choose easy was
about choosing things

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:36.832
that you're naturally or
intrinsically interested in.

00:24:36.832 --> 00:24:38.737
They feel like they
resonate with your values,

00:24:38.737 --> 00:24:41.610
you're good at them or
like, I actually think

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:42.670
that's the part that you did.

00:24:42.670 --> 00:24:44.880
You chose something that
you're very, very good at

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:47.480
and committed to, it resonates
with your deepest values,

00:24:47.480 --> 00:24:50.560
you love learning, you
probably love teaching. Okay.

00:24:50.560 --> 00:24:51.970
Then what's number two?

00:24:51.970 --> 00:24:53.010
Number two is work hard.

00:24:53.010 --> 00:24:54.590
And number two is you know what?

00:24:54.590 --> 00:24:56.600
The audit is part of running Khan Academy.

00:24:56.600 --> 00:24:57.433
You know what?

00:24:57.433 --> 00:24:58.530
Raising money is part of Khan Academy.

00:24:58.530 --> 00:24:59.915
I'm sure you don't like that either.

00:24:59.915 --> 00:25:02.100
You know what, like doing whatever it is

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:02.933
that you need to do.

00:25:02.933 --> 00:25:04.200
And I think that's where
you're using these like

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:07.160
mental hacks to kind of like
get yourself to be excited

00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:09.280
about an audit or, and
that's the part where

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:12.904
you do need to use discipline
and strategies to work hard.

00:25:12.904 --> 00:25:15.390
And by the way, the third
part is paid forward, right?

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:20.050
So, you know, for every good
person in life, you know,

00:25:20.050 --> 00:25:23.047
that means, you know, doing
things for other people.

00:25:23.047 --> 00:25:25.290
But I do think that sometimes young people

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:28.750
get these confused and they
think they should choose hard

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:29.920
and that the work should be easy.

00:25:29.920 --> 00:25:33.330
But like, no, you choose
things that are kind of fluent

00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:34.370
and fluid for you and then there's always

00:25:34.370 --> 00:25:36.590
going to be friction and
that's when you have to learn

00:25:36.590 --> 00:25:37.990
all these ways to work hard.

00:25:39.201 --> 00:25:40.674
Does that make any sense?

00:25:40.674 --> 00:25:42.560
- No, it made a ton of sense

00:25:42.560 --> 00:25:43.940
and this is a fascinating conversation,

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:45.360
you know, whenever we're having
these good conversations,

00:25:45.360 --> 00:25:48.500
the time goes by far
faster than I would like.

00:25:48.500 --> 00:25:51.340
I hope we have more time
to have this conversation

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:52.950
'cause I think we're just getting started.

00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:56.660
And maybe we have time for one
more question from YouTube,

00:25:56.660 --> 00:25:59.460
Sanjiv Kumar asks, can anyone
develop grit at any age

00:25:59.460 --> 00:26:02.480
or is it more specific for
children and young adults?

00:26:02.480 --> 00:26:05.110
- I really do believe that
one of the great lessons

00:26:05.110 --> 00:26:07.760
of psychological science
over the last few decades

00:26:07.760 --> 00:26:11.520
is that we are remarkably malleable.

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:13.350
In other words, Carol
Dweck is not only right

00:26:13.350 --> 00:26:15.535
about intelligence but about pretty much

00:26:15.535 --> 00:26:17.620
every other facet of human nature.

00:26:17.620 --> 00:26:19.770
And I'm not saying that
you can metamorphose

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:22.830
into like some completely
different human being in a day,

00:26:22.830 --> 00:26:25.500
but I do think, yeah, you
can develop growth mindset,

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:28.091
you can develop grit, you
can become a nicer person,

00:26:28.091 --> 00:26:30.940
you can become a better
listener, you can become

00:26:30.940 --> 00:26:32.700
a little more emotionally,
like all of those things.

00:26:32.700 --> 00:26:35.390
And it's not just childhood
and it's not just adolescence

00:26:35.390 --> 00:26:38.110
and it's not even just early
adulthood or middle adulthood.

00:26:38.110 --> 00:26:42.246
There's not a point in
the entire span of a life

00:26:42.246 --> 00:26:45.350
where people stop learning and growing

00:26:45.350 --> 00:26:47.917
if they're open to it.

00:26:47.917 --> 00:26:50.075
- Wow. I want us to have
a conversation about

00:26:50.075 --> 00:26:52.672
each of those things
'cause I want all of them.

00:26:52.672 --> 00:26:53.505
(laughing)

00:26:53.505 --> 00:26:54.338
For myself.

00:26:54.338 --> 00:26:55.990
- I would love to have
another conversation, Sal.

00:26:55.990 --> 00:26:57.817
You know, I'm a huge fan of Khan Academy

00:26:57.817 --> 00:26:59.210
and I'm a huge fan of yours.

00:26:59.210 --> 00:27:01.950
- Let's book it cause I think
we're just getting started.

00:27:01.950 --> 00:27:04.100
I mean there's so many
questions folks are,

00:27:05.223 --> 00:27:07.560
I'm trying to see if I
could squeeze one more in.

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:12.560
Let's see, this is a big one,
but from YouTube Bilal A asks,

00:27:12.807 --> 00:27:14.097
"Where does passion come from?

00:27:14.097 --> 00:27:16.467
"I am 31 and still trying
to find my passion.

00:27:16.467 --> 00:27:18.107
"I don't find anything interesting enough

00:27:18.107 --> 00:27:19.530
"to put my soul into."

00:27:19.530 --> 00:27:20.363
Any thoughts there?

00:27:20.363 --> 00:27:22.030
I have some thoughts.

00:27:22.030 --> 00:27:23.430
- Okay, well you start with your thoughts

00:27:23.430 --> 00:27:25.870
and then I'll end and this
will be a good last question.

00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:26.940
What's your thought first?

00:27:26.940 --> 00:27:31.150
- My quick advice for Bilal
is make space for exploration.

00:27:31.150 --> 00:27:32.870
I had that same angst when I was young,

00:27:32.870 --> 00:27:35.370
I had good jobs, but
they weren't my passion.

00:27:35.370 --> 00:27:37.100
But I had space for exploration.

00:27:37.100 --> 00:27:39.070
And through that space for exploration,

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:42.390
I was able to find my true passion.

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:44.120
- Okay. That's great advice.

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:45.010
I agree with you.

00:27:45.010 --> 00:27:48.390
And when I was 31 I was crying and crying

00:27:48.390 --> 00:27:50.460
on my living room couch, not knowing

00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:52.350
what I wanted to do
with my life, literally.

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:54.320
I mean, ask my husband.

00:27:54.320 --> 00:27:56.720
The couch was nearly salty with like,

00:27:56.720 --> 00:27:59.270
you know, you could almost see
the precipitate of the salt

00:27:59.270 --> 00:28:00.609
on the surface of the pillows.

00:28:00.609 --> 00:28:04.340
I was crying that much, and
I really like sympathize

00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:07.337
with this feeling of like,
"Oh my gosh, you know,

00:28:07.337 --> 00:28:10.280
"I'm trying to have a passion,
but I don't know what it is."

00:28:10.280 --> 00:28:12.950
And for me it was, you know,
giving myself little freedom

00:28:12.950 --> 00:28:16.170
but I think also it was reflecting on

00:28:16.170 --> 00:28:19.400
what I had become
interested in in the past.

00:28:19.400 --> 00:28:21.990
And when I thought back to
when I was 16 years old,

00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:25.590
I remembered that when I was
16 I went to summer school.

00:28:25.590 --> 00:28:28.440
And because my parents
who were very cheap,

00:28:28.440 --> 00:28:30.770
like wouldn't pay for anything until like

00:28:30.770 --> 00:28:31.690
their kids could pay for it.

00:28:31.690 --> 00:28:33.339
So I had saved up all my
money and the first time

00:28:33.339 --> 00:28:35.030
I could afford to do
anything over the summer

00:28:35.030 --> 00:28:36.040
was when I was 16.

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:38.720
So I got to choose and I
opened up that course catalog.

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:39.553
And what did I choose?

00:28:39.553 --> 00:28:40.990
I chose two classes.

00:28:40.990 --> 00:28:43.840
Psychology and nonfiction writing.

00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:45.960
And that's essentially all I do all day.

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:47.650
Psychology and nonfiction writing.

00:28:47.650 --> 00:28:50.540
So I guess I would suggest
that if you're a 31 year old

00:28:50.540 --> 00:28:53.440
who like me was maybe
crying a lot, angst ridden.

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:55.520
Is that in addition to
giving yourself some space

00:28:55.520 --> 00:29:00.520
for self exploration is,
try to recall in your life

00:29:00.870 --> 00:29:03.760
the times where you were
interested in things

00:29:03.760 --> 00:29:05.670
or you were excited and you might be able

00:29:05.670 --> 00:29:08.360
to piece together some of
the puzzle and I kid you not,

00:29:08.360 --> 00:29:10.430
when I was 32, I just like,
I had thought about it,

00:29:10.430 --> 00:29:12.057
I was like, "Oh, I'm
interested in psychology,

00:29:12.057 --> 00:29:13.227
"I'm interested in writing.

00:29:13.227 --> 00:29:14.060
"I used to teach math.

00:29:14.060 --> 00:29:15.467
"That must be, and I'm pretty good at math

00:29:15.467 --> 00:29:16.677
"or I like math, I don't know.

00:29:16.677 --> 00:29:20.530
"And I think I should be a psychologist."

00:29:20.530 --> 00:29:23.040
And then I went to graduate school.

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:25.423
- Wow. And then you
became Angela Duckworth.

00:29:26.399 --> 00:29:29.510
Well then I learned a lot about
psychology, I'll say that.

00:29:29.510 --> 00:29:33.100
Yeah, I think for me, I would say that now

00:29:33.100 --> 00:29:37.070
my work is my calling and it is who I am.

00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:39.150
I think about it all the time.

00:29:39.150 --> 00:29:40.850
I think about it if I wake up
in the middle of the night,

00:29:40.850 --> 00:29:42.050
I think about it on Sunday morning.

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:43.240
I think about it on Friday night.

00:29:43.240 --> 00:29:45.580
And I think that's what
this 31 year old wants.

00:29:45.580 --> 00:29:46.670
They want that.

00:29:46.670 --> 00:29:47.870
And it was a process.

00:29:47.870 --> 00:29:49.370
I don't want to say it happened, you know,

00:29:49.370 --> 00:29:51.873
in a moment in time, but,
but there was, you know,

00:29:51.873 --> 00:29:53.640
a long period of struggle.

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:57.160
And I do empathize with that and I have

00:29:57.160 --> 00:29:59.375
a lot of hope for you because, you know,

00:29:59.375 --> 00:30:02.140
you too, Sal probably
couldn't have foreseen

00:30:02.140 --> 00:30:05.280
that this would be your
calling, but it is,

00:30:05.280 --> 00:30:06.930
and callings generally, you know,

00:30:06.930 --> 00:30:11.253
take a while to develop
and to deepen over time.

00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:14.660
- No, that's incredible,
incredible advice.

